// Tombstone forms (ridl §11, typl §7.4 grammar `reserved = "reserved"
// ( camelCase_id | int_lit )`).
//
// A `reserved` entry retires an interaction and holds its ordinal for ever.
// `tools/diff/src/classify.rs::slots` is the consumer that matters: it folds
// tombstones into the ordinal sequence so a freed slot never looks free, which
// is what stops a new interaction reusing a retired wire identity from
// classifying as a clean append. That guarantee is what a registry gate rests
// on, so both spellings belong in the golden record — and in both interaction
// stores, since a named interface and a service's inline shape lower through
// separate loops.
//
// The named form appears in `appendix-a.ridl` and `services.ridl` already.
// What is here is the *nameless* form, `reserved <ordinal>`, which lowers to
// `Reserved { name: None, value: Some(n) }` — a slot that records the ordinal
// it protects and nothing else.

package veh.cluster

/// Retired members in a named interface. Ordinals 2 and 4 can never be
/// reissued; `wheelSlipRate` is ordinal 5, not ordinal 3.
interface WheelHistory {
  signal wheelTicks : FanLevel @[100ms..1s]
  reserved legacyWheelPhase
  signal wheelTemp : Temperature @[1s..10s]
  reserved 4
  signal wheelSlipRate : Ratio @[500ms..5s]
}

/// The same two forms inside a service's inline shape.
service veh.cluster.wheels {
  signal ticks : FanLevel @[100ms..1s]
  reserved legacyTicks
  reserved 3
  event slipped : FilterState @[50ms..500ms]
}
